# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill it in. These are the only variables the app
# reads. Never commit a real `.env` — it is gitignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string used by Prisma. Required; the app will not boot
# without it. The value below is a placeholder for a local database — replace
# the credentials, host, and database name with your own.
#
# On Railway, do not paste a connection string here. Add a Postgres database to
# the project and set this as a reference variable on the app service:
#     DATABASE_URL=${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}}
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/flone?schema=public"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email. Required in production (an account cannot sign in until its address
# is confirmed); all optional locally. Use Resend OR any SMTP server. With both
# set, Resend is used.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# API key from https://resend.com/api-keys.
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY IN DEVELOPMENT. With no key the app does not send anything:
# it prints each message to the console instead, including the full
# verification / reset / invitation link, so every flow is clickable straight
# out of `bun run dev` with no Resend account.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Any SMTP server, instead of Resend. Setting SMTP_HOST turns it on.
#
# SMTP_PORT defaults to 587 (STARTTLS). SMTP_SECURE=true means TLS from the
# first byte, and defaults to true on port 465. With SMTP_USER set, a server
# that does not offer TLS is refused rather than sent the password in the clear;
# with it empty (an unauthenticated local relay) plain SMTP is allowed.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_SECURE=

# The From address. Your provider must accept it -- with Resend, its domain must
# be verified -- or every send is rejected. A display name is optional:
# `Flone <no-reply@example.com>`.
EMAIL_FROM="Flone <no-reply@example.com>"

# The public origin, used to build every link in every email. No trailing path.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION, and deliberately not derived from the request. A link
# built out of the Host header is host-header poisoning: an attacker sets the
# header, the victim gets a genuine email from the real system, and the link
# hands the one-time token to the attacker's domain. With a transport set but this
# missing, the app logs `[email] not_configured` and sends nothing rather than
# send a broken link. Defaults to http://localhost:3000 in development.
APP_URL="https://flone.example.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image attachments (an S3-compatible bucket). Optional — with these unset the
# app runs exactly as it did before the feature existed: the paste, drop and
# attach affordances answer 503 with "Image attachments are not configured on
# this server." and nothing else changes.
#
# ALL FOUR OF THE FIRST FOUR ARE REQUIRED TOGETHER. Any one missing counts as
# "no bucket" — a half-configured bucket would fail at upload time with a
# signature error, which reads like a wrong secret rather than a missing one.
#
# On Railway: create a bucket in the project (Storage → Add Bucket), then set
# these on the app service as reference variables so a credential rotation
# needs no redeploy of hand-copied values:
#
#     S3_BUCKET=${{flone-attachments.BUCKET}}
#     S3_ENDPOINT=${{flone-attachments.ENDPOINT}}
#     S3_REGION=${{flone-attachments.REGION}}
#     S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=${{flone-attachments.ACCESS_KEY_ID}}
#     S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=${{flone-attachments.SECRET_ACCESS_KEY}}
#
# Any S3-compatible endpoint works — Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, MinIO, real
# S3. Requests are signed with SigV4 path-style (`<endpoint>/<bucket>/<key>`)
# by `src/lib/bucket.ts`; there is no SDK to configure.
#
# THE BUCKET MUST STAY PRIVATE. Nothing in the app publishes an object URL:
# `GET /api/attachments/<id>` authorizes the caller against the issue and then
# redirects to a URL that expires in five minutes. A public bucket would make
# every screenshot in every organization readable by anyone with the link.
S3_BUCKET=
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# SigV4 needs *a* region in the credential scope; most S3-compatible endpoints
# ignore which one it is. Defaults to us-east-1 when empty.
S3_REGION=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S3_ENDPOINT IS ALSO READ AT BUILD TIME, and this is the one thing about
# attachments worth knowing before it costs an afternoon.
#
# The serve route redirects to the bucket, and a Content-Security-Policy fetch
# directive is re-checked on every redirect hop — so `next.config.ts` adds the
# endpoint's origin to `img-src`. That header is compiled into the build.
#
# Build without S3_ENDPOINT set and uploads still succeed, but every attached
# image is blocked by the CSP: a broken-image icon and a console violation, with
# no server-side error anywhere. On Railway a service variable is present at
# build as well as at runtime, so the reference variables above are enough.
# Locally, `bun run dev` re-reads the config on boot — just restart it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reminders (the scheduler). Required for reminder emails to be sent at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The bearer token that guards POST /api/cron/reminders. Generate one with:
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# WITH THIS UNSET THE ENDPOINT IS CLOSED and no reminder is ever delivered —
# in development too. An unauthenticated trigger is a way for anyone to make
# this instance send mail, and "it is only development" is how that ships.
#
# Point any scheduler at the endpoint. Extra and overlapping calls are safe:
# each send is claimed with a compare-and-swap, so a reminder is delivered at
# most once however often the tick runs. The tick interval is the WORST-CASE
# LATENESS of a reminder — every 5 minutes means a 09:00 reminder arrives
# between 09:00 and 09:05.
#
#     curl -fsS -X POST -H "authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" \
#       https://flone.example.com/api/cron/reminders
#
# On Railway: deploy `services/cron` as a cron service with a `*/5 * * * *`
# schedule (5 minutes is Railway's floor) and CRON_SECRET / APP_URL as
# reference variables to this service. Do NOT put the curl call above in a
# Railway start command instead: start commands are split into argv without a
# shell, so `$CRON_SECRET` reaches curl as those literal characters and every
# run is a 401 that reads like a wrong secret. See services/cron/README.md.
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reminder phone calls (the voice service). Optional — leave both empty and
# reminders are email-only, exactly as they were before the feature existed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where `services/voice` is listening. NO TRAILING SLASH and no path: the app
# appends `/call` itself.
#
# On Railway this service has NO PUBLIC DOMAIN. It is reached over the private
# network, which is the point — nothing on the internet can route to a process
# whose entire job is to dial phone numbers:
#
#     VOICE_URL=http://voice.railway.internal:3001
#
# Locally: `cd services/voice && bun index.ts` and use http://localhost:3001.
VOICE_URL=

# The bearer token the app presents to the voice service. Generate one with:
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# It must match VOICE_SECRET on the voice service itself. Unlike CRON_SECRET
# this one CAN be a Railway reference variable (`${{voice.VOICE_SECRET}}`) —
# the expansion failure documented above only affects start commands, not
# environment variables, so rotating it is a normal variable change.
#
# WITH THIS UNSET NO CALL IS EVER PLACED, and the voice service refuses every
# request. A dialer whose only protection is "nobody can route to it" becomes an
# open dialer the first time somebody generates a domain to debug something.
VOICE_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The voice service's OWN variables. These belong on that service, NOT here —
# they are listed for reference because the whole point of the split is that
# the app never holds a carrier credential.
#
#   TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID   from console.twilio.com, starts `AC`
#   TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN    same panel, click to reveal
#   TWILIO_FROM          a Voice-capable number you own, E.164: +18506696996
#   VOICE_SECRET         the same value as above
#   PORT                 set by Railway; defaults to 3001
#
# Two Twilio settings cost a failed call each to discover, both in the console:
#   - Voice → Settings → Geographic Permissions must have the DESTINATION
#     country ticked. It is off by default for most of the world, and the
#     failure is error 21215, which reads like a malformed number;
#   - a TRIAL account can only call numbers added under Phone Numbers → Manage
#     → Verified Caller IDs (error 21219), and prepends a trial notice to every
#     call before your message plays.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional — you almost never set this by hand. Next.js sets it for you:
# "development" for `bun run dev`, "production" for `bun run build` / `bun run start`.
# It is read in exactly two places: the Prisma client singleton (dev-only global
# caching) and the session cookie (production switches the cookie to the
# `__Host-` prefix and turns on the Secure flag).
# NODE_ENV="development"
